  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 AM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nDry and quiet weather conditions will be on tap for today with \ndaytime highs slightly above normal. Meanwhile current water vapor \nsatellite imagery shows a lead short wave over southern California \nwith the main upper-level wave over northern California. The lead \nshort wave will race northeast across the Rockies today then reach \nwestern Kansas by late tonight. This will spark off elevated \nshowers/storms over western Kansas tonight which will then spread \neast into central/south central Kansas between 3am-6am. Elevated \nshear/instability combination is supportive for a few possible \nstrong storms late tonight. Otherwise the line of showers/storms \nwill continue to move slowly east Monday morning reaching southeast \nKansas in the afternoon. The environment east of the Kansas turnpike \ncould remain elevated with a few strong storms possible in the \nafternoon. Otherwise the main focus area we will be monitoring for \nsurface based storm re-development is for locations west of a Salina \nto Wichita, to Wellington line between 3pm-6pm. This is where high \nresolution models(HREF/REFS) show increasing probabilities for storm \ninitiation. Surface-6km wind shear and 0-1km SRH are favorable for \norganized storm development capable of producing severe weather with \na few tornadoes possible. Shear orientation vector is parallel to \ninitiating low level boundary which would cause storms to grow \nupscale into a linear storm mode with damaging winds becoming the \nmain hazard. Once again the key thing to watch will be timing and \namount of clearing for the low clouds to help destabilize the \natmosphere. The line of storms will move east into southeast Kansas \nduring the evening hours on Monday.\n\nUpper level wave will shift northeast into eastern Nebraska on \nTuesday with deformation wrap around rain showers in the morning \nover central Kansas along with gusty northwest winds. Dry weather \nexpected on Wednesday with models showing another system racing east \nacross the central plains on Thursday. Moisture will be limited with \nthis system which keeps possible precipitation chances rather low. \nLong range models are showing a healthy upper-level wave diving \nsoutheast across the central plains on Saturday which could bring \nanother chance of rain to Kansas.",
